project:
  name: Makuli
  description: A personalized weekly meal planning app that generates 7-day meal plans and grocery lists based on a user’s dietary goals, preferences, and budget. Built for iOS using SwiftUI and Supabase.
  
  tech_stack:
    frontend: SwiftUI (MVVM architecture)
    backend: Supabase (PostgreSQL, Auth, Storage, RLS)
    auth: Supabase Auth (email/password)
    storage: Supabase Storage (for recipe images)
    database: Supabase PostgreSQL with UUIDs, constraints, and enum validations
    data_format: JSON
    architecture: MVVM (Models, Views, ViewModels)

  mvp_features:
    - Onboarding
    - User Profile Management
    - AI/Template-based Meal Plan Generation
    - Recipe Browser
    - Weekly Meal Plan Display (by day + meal type)
    - Grocery List Generation
    - Preference/Goal Editing
    - Supabase-integrated Backend

  features:
    onboarding:
      description: First-time experience to capture user budget, dietary goals, cuisine preferences.
      implementation:
        - SwiftUI Views with `NavigationStack`
        - Store responses locally using `@AppStorage` or in Supabase via `profiles` table
        - Sync to Supabase on completion with `SupabaseClient.auth.currentUser.id` as FK

    user_profile:
      description: Displays user goals, preferences, and budget. Editable.
      implementation:
        - Model: `UserProfile`
        - Editable fields: budget, calories, dietary tags, allergies
        - Update via ViewModel method `updateUserProfile()`, which calls Supabase `update`

    meal_plan_generation:
      description: Creates a 7-day meal plan based on user preferences.
      types:
        - template: Predefined plans
        - ai: AI-generated (future feature)
        - manual: User-curated
      implementation:
        - Call `generateMealPlan()` in ViewModel
        - Choose 3 meals/day: breakfast, lunch, dinner
        - Select from `recipes` table based on filters (tags, budget, calories)
        - Save to `plans` table with entries in `plan_recipes`
        - Uses Supabase RPC or local logic (currently client-side Swift)

    recipe_browser:
      description: View all available recipes with images, ingredients, macros, and tags.
      implementation:
        - Fetch from `recipes` table
        - Display with async image loading via `Supabase Storage`
        - Tappable cards → Recipe Detail View

    meal_plan_display:
      description: Clean weekly view of user's current plan, grouped by day and meal type.
      implementation:
        - Query `plan_recipes` joined with `recipes` for a given plan
        - Organize with `ForEach(days)` and `Section(header:)`
        - ViewModel caches structured plan data to avoid repeated fetches

    grocery_list:
      description: Auto-generated list of all ingredients in a plan, de-duplicated and grouped.
      implementation:
        - Aggregate ingredients from all recipes in the current plan
        - Flatten and normalize units (e.g., g, ml)
        - Group by category (if tagged), and allow users to check off items
        - Stored locally or synced to Supabase under `grocery_items`

    preferences_and_goals:
      description: User can change goals, budget, and preferences anytime.
      implementation:
        - `UserProfileView` allows editing
        - Update triggers plan regeneration prompt
        - Changes persisted to Supabase in `profiles` table

  database_models:
    - profiles (user_id PK/FK, budget, calories, tags, etc.)
    - recipes (id, title, image_url, ingredients[], instructions[], tags[], estimated_cost)
    - plans (id, user_id, title, start_date, end_date, total_cost, generation_method ['template', 'ai', 'manual'])
    - plan_recipes (plan_id, recipe_id, day_of_week, meal_type, order)
    - grocery_items (id, user_id, name, quantity, unit, checked)
  
  dos:
    - DO ensure all foreign keys match real IDs (e.g., recipe_id exists in recipes)
    - DO separate concerns: data fetching in service layer, state in ViewModel, UI in Views
    - DO use `@StateObject` for ViewModels in navigation hierarchies
    - DO verify `generation_method` matches enum ['template', 'ai', 'manual']
    - DO document RPCs if you add any Supabase functions or views

  donts:
    - DON’T hardcode IDs (use UUID())
    - DON’T fetch or mutate directly in SwiftUI Views
    - DON’T bypass RLS or constraints in Supabase
    - DON’T assume recipe cost without checking `estimated_cost`
    - DON’T store large arrays or JSON blobs in local state unnecessarily

  cursor_behaviors:
    safe_autocomplete: true
    smart_refactor: true
    cautious_write: true
    confirm_before_major_change: true
    match_mvvm_pattern: true
    use_supabase_sdk_for_all_db_ops: true
